WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.240
Imagine a small cup of warm water and a large bucket at exactly the same

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:06.660
temperature.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.920
Does warming them by ten degrees require the same energy?

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.440
Of course not.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.740
The bucket contains much more water.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.300
Temperature is not a measure of how much energy we must supply.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.060
Heat means energy transferred because of a temperature difference.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.180
Internal energy belongs to the system; heat describes a transfer.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.360
For a solid or liquid with approximately constant specific heat and no change

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.320
of phase, the energy supplied is mass times specific heat times temperature

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:35.820
change.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.560
Specific heat tells us how much energy one kilogram needs for a one kelvin

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.020
rise.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.060
Heat capacity belongs to the whole sample, so it is mass times specific heat.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.720
Let's heat half a kilogram of water by ten kelvin, using a given specific heat

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.320
of four thousand two hundred joules per kilogram per kelvin.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.860
Half times four thousand two hundred times ten is twenty one thousand joules,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:59.400
or twenty one kilojoules.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.080
Double the mass and we need twice the energy.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.160
Use the temperature change, not the final temperature.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.220
A change in degrees Celsius has the same numerical value in kelvin.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.020
This equation describes sensible heating; we need a different term when a

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:14.920
substance melts or boils.

00:01:15.264 --> 00:01:19.284
Now let's mix hot and cold water in an insulated cup.

00:01:20.004 --> 00:01:24.904
We assume no evaporation, no phase change and negligible energy absorbed by the

00:01:24.904 --> 00:01:25.924
cup for this first example.

00:01:26.664 --> 00:01:30.484
The hot water loses energy and the cold water gains the same amount.

00:01:31.144 --> 00:01:35.184
That is conservation of energy, not a rule about simply averaging two

00:01:35.184 --> 00:01:35.564
temperatures.

00:01:36.504 --> 00:01:40.544
Mix zero point two kilograms of water at eighty degrees Celsius with zero point

00:01:40.544 --> 00:01:42.524
three kilograms at twenty degrees Celsius.

00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:44.864
Call the final temperature T.

00:01:45.864 --> 00:01:49.484
The hot sample loses zero point two times c times eighty minus T.

00:01:50.284 --> 00:01:54.564
The cold sample gains zero point three times c times T minus twenty.

00:01:54.564 --> 00:01:54.964
Equate them.

00:01:55.744 --> 00:01:58.504
Since both samples are water, the same specific heat cancels.

00:01:59.184 --> 00:02:02.964
We get sixteen minus zero point two T equals zero point three T minus six.

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:07.404
So twenty two equals zero point five T, giving forty four degrees Celsius.

00:02:08.464 --> 00:02:09.764
Pause and check whether that is sensible.

00:02:10.244 --> 00:02:14.124
It lies between twenty and eighty, and it is closer to twenty because there is

00:02:14.124 --> 00:02:14.824
more cold water.

00:02:15.564 --> 00:02:18.004
The simple average, fifty degrees, would be wrong.

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:21.724
For different substances, weight the temperatures by their heat capacities,

00:02:22.424 --> 00:02:24.844
mass times specific heat, rather than by mass alone.

00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:29.484
A final temperature outside the initial temperature range is a warning sign in

00:02:29.484 --> 00:02:30.504
this simple mixing model.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:32.924
Our cup was ignored in the last problem.

00:02:33.644 --> 00:02:37.644
Real calorimeters also change temperature, so let's give the cup its share of

00:02:37.644 --> 00:02:38.084
the energy.

00:02:38.904 --> 00:02:43.044
Suppose a hot metal sample is placed in cooler water and both water and cup

00:02:43.044 --> 00:02:44.304
begin at the same temperature.

00:02:44.984 --> 00:02:48.304
The metal's energy loss must warm the water and the cup together.

00:02:49.204 --> 00:02:53.684
Add the water's heat capacity to the cup's heat capacity before multiplying by

00:02:53.684 --> 00:02:54.584
their temperature rise.

00:02:55.344 --> 00:02:56.644
Here is a compact example.

00:02:56.964 --> 00:03:01.864
A zero point one kilogram metal sample has specific heat four hundred joules

00:03:01.864 --> 00:03:03.124
per kilogram per kelvin.

00:03:03.924 --> 00:03:06.804
It cools from one hundred to thirty degrees Celsius.

00:03:07.744 --> 00:03:12.164
Its heat capacity is forty joules per kelvin, and a seventy kelvin fall

00:03:12.164 --> 00:03:14.424
releases two thousand eight hundred joules.

00:03:15.124 --> 00:03:19.964
If the water has heat capacity two hundred and forty joules per kelvin, and the

00:03:19.964 --> 00:03:24.764
cup has forty, their combined heat capacity is two hundred and eighty.

00:03:24.764 --> 00:03:29.444
Starting at twenty degrees, they need two hundred and eighty times ten, again

00:03:29.444 --> 00:03:32.104
two thousand eight hundred joules, to reach thirty degrees.

00:03:32.684 --> 00:03:33.864
The energy balance checks.

00:03:34.664 --> 00:03:37.244
You may see a calorimeter described by its water equivalent.

00:03:37.924 --> 00:03:42.624
That is the mass of water with the same heat capacity as the vessel: divide the

00:03:42.624 --> 00:03:45.104
vessel's heat capacity by water's specific heat.

00:03:45.664 --> 00:03:50.064
It is a convenient comparison, not extra water secretly inside the apparatus.

00:03:50.352 --> 00:03:53.172
Can a substance absorb energy without becoming hotter?

00:03:53.992 --> 00:03:54.312
Yes.

00:03:55.192 --> 00:03:59.312
During melting or boiling of a pure substance at fixed pressure, energy changes

00:03:59.312 --> 00:04:02.392
its phase while the temperature stays at the transition value.

00:04:03.052 --> 00:04:05.192
That is why a heating curve has plateaus.

00:04:05.892 --> 00:04:09.252
The required energy is mass times the specific latent heat.

00:04:09.732 --> 00:04:13.832
Let's turn ice at minus ten degrees Celsius into water at twenty degrees.

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:19.762
Take mass zero point one kilograms, ice specific heat two thousand

00:04:19.762 --> 00:04:24.452
one hundred, fusion latent heat three hundred and thirty four thousand joules

00:04:24.452 --> 00:04:29.777
per kilogram, and water specific heat four thousand two

00:04:29.777 --> 00:04:30.752
hundred.

00:04:30.752 --> 00:04:31.652
There are three stages.

00:04:32.352 --> 00:04:35.652
First warm the ice by ten kelvin: two point one kilojoules.

00:04:36.392 --> 00:04:39.852
Next melt it at zero degrees: thirty three point four kilojoules.

00:04:40.692 --> 00:04:44.372
Finally warm the melted water by twenty kelvin: eight point four kilojoules.

00:04:44.372 --> 00:04:47.152
Add them to get forty three point nine kilojoules.

00:04:48.112 --> 00:04:51.232
Don't multiply the ice specific heat by the entire thirty degree interval.

00:04:52.072 --> 00:04:55.252
The material changes phase, and its specific heat changes too.

00:04:56.212 --> 00:05:00.212
For steam that condenses and then cools, include the condensation energy before

00:05:00.212 --> 00:05:00.992
cooling the liquid.

00:05:01.692 --> 00:05:06.112
The plateau rule assumes the stated pressure and a pure substance; it is not a

00:05:06.112 --> 00:05:09.672
universal claim that every material changes phase at one fixed temperature.

00:05:10.272 --> 00:05:15.172
Here's the step that saves many ice mixture questions: check the phase before

00:05:15.172 --> 00:05:16.692
solving for the final temperature.

00:05:17.572 --> 00:05:22.612
Place zero point zero five kilograms of ice at zero degrees into zero point one

00:05:22.612 --> 00:05:27.132
kilograms of water at twenty degrees, in an insulated container of negligible

00:05:27.132 --> 00:05:27.872
heat capacity.

00:05:29.052 --> 00:05:33.649
Use water specific heat four thousand two hundred and fusion latent heat three

00:05:33.649 --> 00:05:35.732
hundred and thirty four thousand.

00:05:35.732 --> 00:05:39.192
Cooling the water to zero releases eight thousand four hundred joules.

00:05:39.712 --> 00:05:43.412
Melting all the ice would require sixteen thousand seven hundred joules.

00:05:44.232 --> 00:05:45.072
There isn't enough energy.

00:05:45.532 --> 00:05:49.372
So the equilibrium is at zero degrees, with some ice still present.

00:05:50.352 --> 00:05:54.332
Divide eight thousand four hundred by three hundred and thirty four thousand.

00:05:54.332 --> 00:05:58.472
About zero point zero two five one five kilograms, or twenty five point one

00:05:58.472 --> 00:05:59.952
five grams, melts.

00:06:00.892 --> 00:06:03.492
Roughly twenty four point eight five grams remains as ice.

00:06:03.792 --> 00:06:08.052
A calculation that assumes all the ice melts might produce a negative liquid

00:06:08.052 --> 00:06:08.692
water temperature.

00:06:09.672 --> 00:06:13.652
That is a signal to revisit the assumed final state, not to accept the

00:06:13.652 --> 00:06:14.472
impossible result.

00:06:15.292 --> 00:06:19.452
If available energy exceeds the melting requirement, subtract the latent heat

00:06:19.452 --> 00:06:23.092
first, then use the remaining energy to warm all the liquid water.

00:06:23.852 --> 00:06:26.592
Always account for the container if its heat capacity is given.

00:06:27.412 --> 00:06:31.172
Energy balance plus a phase check is the heart of calorimetry.
